When Can You Trust an Agent’s Own Verifier?Stress-Testing Counterfactual Rationale VerificationUnder Search Pressure and Oracle Access Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: arXiv is now an independent nonprofit! Learn more × Back to arXiv License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license arXiv:2610.22779v1 [cs.AI] 16 Oct 2026 \workshoptitle Who Verifies the Agents? Toward Reliable Agent Development When Can You Trust an Agent’s Own Verifier? Stress-Testing Counterfactual Rationale Verification Under Search Pressure and Oracle Access Jonghyun Lee Affiliation: Yonsei University Email: devjh@yonsei.ac.kr Moonsu Kim Affiliation: Yonsei University Email: moonsu88@yonsei.ac.kr Sunghwan Park Affiliation: Yonsei University Email: patrick.park@yonsei.ac.kr Abstract Automated agent design increasingly trusts a single score to decide whether a change is an improvement — but a score optimized aggressively enough ceases to measure what it was meant to. When an agent also explains its design, that explanation is a second signal we can check without human labels: negate each stated claim, re-run, and observe whether performance drops. We ask whether such a verifier stays reliable once an agent optimizes against it. Our contribution is not a new verifier but a way to stress-test one: a pressure ratio ρ ho measuring how much the verifier steers the agent’s search, and four falsifiable probes covering four failure modes — giving no signal, being too weak to matter, being gamed by stating fewer claims, and being queried before the agent commits. We apply the test to our own verifier, the Rationale Consistency Score (RCS), inside an LLM agent that designs machine-learning pipelines, with one generator model, six tasks, and three seeds. First, in our prior configuration the verifier barely affects the search objective ( ρ ≪ 1 ho\ll 1 ), partly because of an accuracy gate, so its robustness had not been tested; it still shapes the reported artifact through a final-selection tie-break that ρ ho does not register. Second, a capability control shows the agent shortens its claim lists when instructed, yet search does not, even at ρ ho more than 100 × 100	imes the prior level; since feedback omits claim volume and horizons are short, this null result is largely structural rather than evidence of robustness. Third, when the agent can probe the verifier before committing, it keeps claims that pass while held-out faithfulness falls — a decline invisible to in-loop metrics, though partly confounded by how RCS scores small pipelines. We offer these as preliminary evidence and propose a reporting standard: audit a verifier with ρ ho and these probes before trusting it. 1 Introduction Verification is the bottleneck in automated agent design. When an outer loop proposes changes to an agent and an inner loop scores them, the score is the only safeguard separating genuine improvement from silent regression. In practice that score is usually a scalar task reward, and a scalar reward optimized aggressively enough ceases to measure the quantity it was intended to track ( Manheim and Garrabrant, 2018 ; Skalse et al., 2022 ; Gao et al., 2023 ) . Agents that design artifacts often also explain them: an agent proposing a machine-learning pipeline states which features matter and why a model class suits the data. That reasoning is usually discarded, but it has a property the task reward lacks — it is checkable against the environment without human labels . If the agent claims a feature is important, we remove it, retrain, and observe whether performance falls: remove-and-retrain evaluation of attribution methods ( Hooker et al., 2019 ) , applied to natural-language claims rather than saliency maps. The Rationale Consistency Score (RCS) formalizes this check. It is attractive as a verification signal for three reasons: it is grounded in the environment rather than in a learned reward model, it is orthogonal in construction to task accuracy, and it is defined per-claim, so it degrades gracefully when only part of an explanation is faithful. For these same reasons, however, RCS is also a target. Once RCS enters an optimization objective, the agent has an incentive to make claims that survive verification rather than claims that are informative. The simplest such strategy requires no sophistication: state fewer claims — one safe claim, not eight, scores well on an average-over-claims metric while communicating almost nothing. This is specification gaming in its simplest form ( Amodei et al., 2016 ; Pan et al., 2022 ) , undetectable by any evaluation that reports only the verifier’s mean output. This paper asks whether a counterfactual rationale verifier remains reliable when an agent optimizes against it — whether it prevents reward hacking and stays faithful as the agent evolves, separately from whether it improves task performance. Such a signal should be characterized the way a measuring instrument is — by its failure modes, operating range, and behavior under adversarial pressure — before it is recommended as infrastructure. Our contribution is therefore a stress test , not a verifier: a pressure metric and four falsifiable probes for any counterfactual rationale verifier. Our findings are preliminary and mixed: in our prior configuration the verifier is too weak to have been tested (F2); a capability control shows the agent can shorten its claim lists when instructed, yet search never does so even at ρ ≫ 1 ho\gg 1 (F3), a null result largely due to short horizons and feedback that omits claim counts; and the failure that does appear is oracle exploitation (F4). Contributions. • A stress-test methodology for rationale verifiers : four failure modes (Section 5 ), falsifiable probes for them (Section 6 ), and a capability control for F3, all reusable beyond RCS. • The pressure ratio ρ ho (Section 6.1 ), and how an accuracy-gating rule suppresses it: our prior configuration lies at ρ ≈ 0.007 ho\approx 0.007 – 0.089 0.089 (Section 9.1 ), while controlled sweeps reach ρ ≈ 41.7 ho\approx 41.7 – 63.7 63.7 (Section 9.2 ). • A capability control showing that the agent shortens its claim lists when instructed (Section 9.3 ) while search does not, even at ρ ≫ 1 ho\gg 1 and under UCB1 at a doubled base horizon (Section 9.2 ) — a discovery gap, not a demonstrated reward exploit. • Evidence that probe access produces the selection signature of Section 6.3 on four of six datasets at sign level, with held-out fidelity declines that the estimator may partly explain (Section 9.4 ). 2 Related Work Reward hacking and Goodhart’s law. That a proxy optimized aggressively enough ceases to track its target recurs in reward misspecification ( Manheim and Garrabrant, 2018 ; Skalse et al., 2022 ) and reward-model overoptimization ( Gao et al., 2023 ) , and claim minimization is an instance of specification gaming ( Amodei et al., 2016 ; Pan et al., 2022 ) . The closest structural precedent is a learned judge or reward model folded into an objective: LLM judges carry exploitable biases ( Zheng et al., 2023 ; Wang et al., 2024 ) , and policies over-optimize learned rewards ( Gao et al., 2023 ; Coste et al., 2024 ) . RCS replaces the learned judge with environment interventions; we audit it, measuring how much pressure the verifier exerts before asking whether it is gamed. Rationale faithfulness. Whether stated reasoning reflects the underlying computation has motivated a growing literature: chain-of-thought can be systematically unfaithful ( Turpin et al., 2023 ; Lanham et al., 2023 ) , and faithfulness is distinct from plausibility ( Jacovi and Goldberg, 2020 ) . RCS operationalizes it through counterfactual interventions ( Chuang et al., 2026 ) , extending remove-and-retrain evaluation from saliency maps ( Hooker et al., 2019 ) and feature attribution ( Ribeiro et al., 2016 ; Lundberg and Lee, 2017 ) to natural-language claims. Such removal-based metrics are themselves gameable under optimization ( Hsia et al., 2024 ) ; RCS inherits that exposure, which is why we audit it under search pressure rather than assuming robustness. LLM-driven pipeline design. Automated pipeline construction has moved from search- and ensemble-based systems ( Olson and Moore, 2016 ; Erickson et al., 2020 ) to LLM optimizers ( Yang et al., 2024 ) and proposers that suggest features in natural language ( Hollmann et al., 2023 ) . Our agent adds a verifier over its stated reasons — precisely what creates the gaming surface we study. 3 Setting The design task. We instantiate automated agent design in a constrained but fully executable environment: the agent designs supervised learning pipelines P = ( S , M , H ) P=(S,M,H) — a feature subset S ⊆ F S\subseteq F , a model class M M , and hyperparameters H H — with task performance Q ⁡ ( P ) ∈ [ 0 , 1 ] Q(P)\in[0,1] estimated as Q ^ ​ ( P ) \hat{Q}(P) on validation data. The setting is narrow relative to open-ended agentic tasks (Section 10 ), but every claim the agent makes has a mechanically constructible counterfactual, which makes the verifier study feasible. The agent. The agent is a large language model inside an optimization loop. In the default proposer — the one used for the main configuration, the pressure sweeps, and the capability control — each iteration is a single call that returns a feature set, a model, hyperparameters, and a natural-language rationale E P E_{P} . The tool-using proposer, which may call list_features , optionally probe_feature_drop , and then submit_pipeline , appears only in the probe-access probe (Section 6.3 ), where both arms use it. Proposals are executed, scored, and used to update Thompson sampling posteriors over pipeline-type arms ( Agrawal and Goyal, 2012 ; Kaufmann et al., 2012 ) , whose recommendations are fed back into the next prompt. Claims. The rationale E P E_{P} is parsed into a set of claims E P = { C 1 , … , C k } E_{P}=\{C_{1},\dots,C_{k}\} of three kinds: feature-importance claims, model-choice claims, and hyperparameter claims. Parsing is deterministic and does not depend on the verification machinery, a property we rely on in Section 7 . Design Agent Verifier Agent Search Agent LLM proposer Tool interface list_features probe_feature_drop Submit ( S , M , H ) + E P (S,M,H)+E_{P} Claim parser F3 Counterfactual constructor Executor (retrain) RCS ^ \widehat{\mathrm{RCS}} F1 R = Q ^ + λ t ​ RCS ^ R=\hat{Q}+\lambda_{t}\,\widehat{\mathrm{RCS}} F2 Thompson Sampling reward artifact F4 oracle Q ^ \hat{Q} RCS ^ \widehat{\mathrm{RCS}} next iteration within-agent / scored messages F4 oracle channel search feedback Figure 1: Multi-agent architecture of the stress-test loop. The Design Agent proposes a pipeline and rationale in a single call, and through the tool interface only in the P2 arms (Section 6.3 ); the Verifier Agent parses claims, builds counterfactuals, and returns RCS ^ \widehat{\mathrm{RCS}} ; the Search Agent forms R = Q ^ + λ t ​ RCS ^ R=\hat{Q}+\lambda_{t}\widehat{\mathrm{RCS}} and updates Thompson Sampling. Cross-agent edges carry the artifact, oracle probes (F4, oracle arm only), task/verifier scores, and the next-iteration recommendation. Tags F1–F4 mark where each protocol probe attaches. 4 RCS as a Verifier Provenance. RCS as defined in Equation 1 is introduced here; we are not auditing a third-party standard. The system we instrument is our own research agent, and “deployed” throughout means our prior configuration of that agent — including the accuracy-gating rule of Section 6.1 — so the F2 measurement is a self-audit and a motivating case, not a survey finding. Definition 1 (Rationale Consistency Score) . For each claim C ∈ E P C\in E_{P} , let P ¬ C P^{
