eg C})ight). (1) Counterfactuals are constructed per claim type: a feature-importance claim is negated by removing the feature and retraining; a model-choice claim by substituting an alternative model class; a hyperparameter claim by perturbing that hyperparameter. When three or more feature-importance claims are stated, the estimator groups them into one joint intervention (Appendix A ). What RCS verifies, and what it does not. RCS asks whether the agent’s stated reasons are load-bearing for the artifact it produced. It does not ask whether the artifact performs well, and it does not certify that a change to the agent improved capability. It is therefore a heterogeneous signal complementing outcome-based verification rather than replacing it. Estimator variants. Replacing Q Q by the empirical Q ^ \hat{Q} gives the base estimator RCS ^ base ​ ( P ) = 1 | E P | ​ ∑ C ∈ E P max ⁡ ( 0 , Q ^ ​ ( P ) − Q ^ ​ ( P ¬ C ) ) , \widehat{\mathrm{RCS}}_{	ext{base}}(P)\;=\; rac{1}{|E_{P}|}\sum_{C\in E_{P}}\max\!\left(0,\;\hat{Q}(P)-\hat{Q}(P^{
eg C})ight), (2) which is sensitive to validation noise. The deployed system uses a practical variant with a small modality-dependent noise threshold, a relative-drop boost, and an amplification threshold (Appendix A ); Equations 1 – 2 idealize it; all results concern this deployed variant, abbreviated RCS ^ \widehat{\mathrm{RCS}} . 5 How a Rationale Verifier Fails We distinguish four failure modes. The first two concern the verifier as an instrument; the last two concern the agent’s response to it. They follow from the structure of the setting rather than from RCS’s internals (though tested only on RCS): F2 applies to any auxiliary term in a composite objective, F3 to any per-claim average whose denominator the agent controls, and F4 to any verifier exposed as a callable tool. F1: Silence. The verifier returns no usable signal. Every counterfactual drop falls below the noise threshold, or no claims could be parsed, and the reported score is a floor value rather than a measurement. We treat the floor rate as a primary reported quantity. F2: Untested. The verifier appears in the objective but contributes so little relative to task-reward variation that the agent’s search is effectively unchanged. Observing no gaming under these conditions is uninformative about robustness: the incentive was never applied. F3: Claim minimization. Suppose k c k_{c} of the | E P | |E_{P}| claims are counterfactually impactful. Taking expectations in Equation 1 then gives 𝔼 ⁡ [ RCS ⁡ ( P ) ] = k c | E P | ​ Δ ¯ c , \mathbb{E}\!\left[\mathrm{RCS}(P)ight]\;=\; rac{k_{c}}{|E_{P}|}\, ar{\Delta}_{c}, (3) with Δ ¯ c  ar{\Delta}_{c} the mean drop over impactful claims. The score depends on the ratio of impactful to stated claims, so deleting one inert claim multiplies it by | E P | / ( | E P | − 1 ) |E_{P}|/(|E_{P}|-1) while leaving the artifact untouched. This holds for RCS \mathrm{RCS} ; the deployed RCS ^ \widehat{\mathrm{RCS}} groups feature claims and averages over executed interventions (Appendix A ), so deleting a stated claim can leave RCS ^ \widehat{\mathrm{RCS}} unchanged. We test whether search discovers shorter claim lists under pressure (P1) and whether the agent produces them when instructed (P4). F4: Oracle exploitation. If the agent can query the verifier before committing, it can filter its claims to those that pass. The resulting rationale is verified but selected. Distinguishing genuine improvement from selection requires observing claim volume alongside pass rate. 6 A Stress-Test Protocol Each probe states a treatment, a control, and a falsifying observation. The mapping is not one-to-one: F2 is read directly off ρ ho , P1 and P4 both target F3, P2 targets F4, and P3 targets F1. 6.1 Auditing Verifier Pressure Let the search objective be R ⁡ ( P ) = Q ^ ​ ( P ) + λ ⋅ RCS ^ ​ ( P ) R(P)=\hat{Q}(P)+\lambda\cdot\widehat{\mathrm{RCS}}(P) . We define the pressure ratio over a run of T T iterations: ρ = 1 T ​ ∑ t = 1 T λ t ⋅ RCS ^ ​ ( P t ) sd ⁡ ( { Q ^ ​ ( P t ) } t = 1 T ) , ho\;=\; rac{ rac{1}{T}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\lambda_{t}\cdot\widehat{\mathrm{RCS}}(P_{t})}{\mathrm{sd}\!\left(\{\hat{Q}(P_{t})\}_{t=1}^{T}ight)}, (4) where λ t \lambda_{t} is the weight actually applied at iteration t t . When ρ ≪ 1 ho\ll 1 the verifier has negligible influence on search. We report ρ ho for every run. Three claims of decreasing generality. (i) Applied pressure must be measured : in any composite objective, whether an auxiliary term steers the search is not readable from its nominal weight. This is our primary methodological claim. (ii) ρ ho is one operationalization of it. Its denominator is a design choice that Section 10 shows can inflate, and its numerator is a time average, so pressure concentrated in a few iterations is indistinguishable from uniformly small pressure; the selection flip rate we report beside it (Table 4 ) is exposed to neither. (iii) The ρ ≈ 0.007 ho\approx 0.007 – 0.089 0.089 comes from a single self-audit , not a survey, and partly reflects the gate below; ungated at λ nom = 0.12 \lambda_{\mathrm{nom}}=0.12 , the sweep level nearest the global nominal 0.1 0.1 , it is 0.04 0.04 – 0.28 0.28 (Table 7 ). Why nominal weights mislead. The system first scales the nominal weight, then gates it on validation accuracy (Appendix A ). On Credit-G, λ ¯ t = 0.030  ar{\lambda}_{t}=0.030 versus s d ​ λ nom = 0.088 s_{d}\lambda_{\mathrm{nom}}=0.088 (global nominal 0.1 0.1 ), a factor of 0.34 0.34 : a plausible gate, not chosen to lower ρ ho , silently reduces the applied weight. Controlled sweeps disable it but retain scaling: R ⁡ ( P ) = Q ^ ​ ( P ) + s d ​ λ nom ​ RCS ^ ​ ( P ) R(P)=\hat{Q}(P)+s_{d}\lambda_{\mathrm{nom}}\widehat{\mathrm{RCS}}(P) , where s d ∈ [ 0.78 , 0.98 ] s_{d}\in[0.78,0.98] . 6.2 P1: Dose–Response over Verifier Pressure Question. Does increasing verifier pressure cause claim minimization (F3)? Design. Sweep nominal λ nom ∈ { 0 , 0.12 , 1 , 5 , 20 } \lambda_{\mathrm{nom}}\in\{0,0.12,1,5,20\} with all else fixed, three seeds per arm; the λ nom = 0 \lambda_{\mathrm{nom}}=0 arm still computes RCS ^ \widehat{\mathrm{RCS}} and only omits it from the reward. Falsification. Declining claim counts with λ nom \lambda_{\mathrm{nom}} is evidence of gaming; stable counts at ρ ≫ 1 ho\gg 1 support robustness. 6.3 P2: Access to the Agent’s Own Verifier Question. When an agent can query the verifier before committing, does its rationale become more faithful or merely better selected (F4)? Design. Both arms use the tool-using proposer and may call list_features ; only the treatment may call probe_feature_drop , capped at four calls per iteration. Interpretation, stated before the results. In Equation 5 , Δ ​ v ^ > 0 \Delta\hat{v}>0 with Δ ​ N ¯ ≈ 0 \Delta ar{N}\approx 0 suggests better reasoning; the displayed conjunction is our diagnostic selection signature. 6.4 P3: Reliability and Disclosure of Failure Question. How often does the verifier return no signal (F1), and how stable is it across seeds? Design. Repeat the main configuration over three seeds and report bootstrap CIs of held-out RCS and task accuracy together with the floor rate. Held-out RCS is measured post-hoc for all arms, including those whose objective excludes RCS. 6.5 P4: Capability Control for Claim Minimization Question. Can the agent reduce claims when the minimization incentive is made explicit? Design. At fixed nominal λ nom = 5 \lambda_{\mathrm{nom}}=5 , three seeds per policy, compare a default prompt with a minimize prompt that both discloses the averaging scoring rule and instructs the agent to state fewer claims while preserving accuracy. Because this arm alters the agent’s information and its instruction together, it bounds capability rather than isolating it: a reduction shows minimization is reachable , not that instruction alone would suffice. If minimize reduces claims while search under P1 does not, F3’s absence under search reflects a discovery gap rather than an inability. 7 Instrumentation For arm a a with T a T_{a} recorded iterations, let n a , t = | E P a , t | n_{a,t}=|E_{P_{a,t}}| be parsed claims, m a , t m_{a,t} tested claims, and z a , t z_{a,t} tested claims with positive counterfactual drop. We report N ¯ a = 1 T a ​ ∑ t = 1 T a n a , t , v ^ a = ∑ t z a , t ∑ t m a , t , Δ ​ N ¯ < 0 ∧ Δ ​ v ^ > 0 ⟹ selection signature ,  ar{N}_{a}= rac{1}{T_{a}}\sum_{t=1}^{T_{a}}n_{a,t},\qquad\hat{v}_{a}= rac{\sum_{t}z_{a,t}}{\sum_{t}m_{a,t}},\qquad\Delta ar{N}<0\ \wedge\ \Delta\hat{v}>0\ \Longrightarrow\ 	ext{selection signature}, (5) where Δ ​ x = x treatment − x control \Delta x=x_{\mathrm{treatment}}-x_{\mathrm{control}} ; v ^ a \hat{v}_{a} is N/A if its denominator is zero. We compute n a , t n_{a,t} independently of verification and also log applied λ t \lambda_{t} , floor vs. measurement, tool/probe calls, and submission path. Feedback exposes task and verifier scores but not n a , t n_{a,t} , so reward has no direct path to claim volume. Released code, prompts, parser, and per-run logs make ρ ho and every probe recomputable. 8 Experimental Setup We run the protocol on six supervised design tasks spanning four modalities (Table 1 ). Each dataset is split 64 / 16 / 20 64/16/20 into train, validation, and test with preprocessing fit on the training partition only. Search uses validation accuracy; all figures labeled held-out are computed once on the test partition after selection. The generator is a single commercial instruction-tuned model, Solar Pro 3 of Upstage’s SOLAR lineage ( Kim et al., 2024 ) , accessed through its hosted API with default decoding parameters and fixed across all arms, so behavioral differences are attributable to the objective and tool access rather than the model. Each run executes 16–19 iterations depending on dataset budget. All confidence intervals are percentile bootstrap intervals over per-seed run means ( 10,000 10{,}000 resamples). All probes use three seeds ( 42 , 43 , 44 42,43,44 ). Across the main configuration, pressure sweep (P1), probe-access probe (P2), and capability control (P4) this yields 180 runs; one run of the main configuration produces both the RCS-included and RCS-excluded arms. The UCB1 sweep in Section 9.2 adds a further 90. Identical-configuration replicate. Because every run resamples the generator, bootstrap intervals over per-seed means miss run-to-run variation at a fixed seed. Two arms of our design are the same configuration executed twice, which bounds that component (Table 3 , Appendix C ): claim volume differs by up to 11.4 % 11.4\% , held-out RCS by × 0.61 	imes 0.61 – × 2.71 	imes 2.71 , test accuracy by up to 0.048 0.048 , and verified-claim rate — the one component the table does not list — by up to 0.06 0.06 . We read every effect below against this band. Table 1: Design tasks and modalities; sources and feature construction are in Appendix B . n n counts the rows actually ingested, which for four tasks exceeds the canonical size by two. Dataset Modality n n d d Notes